1. Executive Summary & Product Vision
Zero-Text Multimodal Desktop Search (Idea #idea_84e84c6c) is a local, visual-first "Second Brain" desktop search engine designed to index and retrieve images from unstructured local directories, including messy image folders, lecture screenshots, and meme dumps. Rather than relying on manual file naming or folder hierarchies, the application enables users to search across thousands of local image assets by semantic "vibe" (natural language), rough hand-drawn sketch, dominant color palette, embedded text (OCR), or visual similarity (image-to-image), alongside an interactive 2D/3D embedding galaxy explorer.
2. Problem Statement & Target Use Cases
Users accumulate thousands of heterogeneous visual files across local directories—such as lecture slides captured during classes, code screenshots, reference diagrams, design inspiration, and meme dumps—with generic filenames (e.g., IMG_4021.png or Screenshot_2026-11-05.png). Traditional operating system file search fails when filenames lack descriptive metadata or when a user only remembers the visual layout, dominant color scheme, or approximate concept of an image.
Core User Scenarios
Semantic & "Vibe" Retrieval: Searching by natural language descriptions of mood, theme, or visual content without requiring exact text matches in filenames.
Lecture Screenshot & Meme Retrieval: Combining Optical Character Recognition (OCR) with semantic embeddings so users can locate lecture diagrams or memes by either embedded text fragments or visual concepts.
Sketch-to-Image Querying: Drawing a rough composition or shape on a live sketch-pad to retrieve visually and structurally matching local images.
Color Palette & Visual Similarity Filtering: Filtering results by dominant color palettes or selecting an existing image to find visually similar assets and near-duplicates.
Visual Discovery (2D/3D Galaxy Explorer): Navigating the entire local image library spatially in a 2D/3D coordinate projection where visually and semantically related images cluster together automatically.
